window.docContents[155].resolve({"/Rings-of-integers/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Rings-of-integers":{"contents":"\n\nThe order  (Zsqrtd). This is the project-owned model\nof , sitting inside\nthe standard quadratic field. It is not\nmathlib's Zsqrtd; the bridge to mathlib's model is intentionally thin and\nisolated.\n\nThe half-integral order \n(ZOnePlusSqrtOverTwo). ZOnePlusSqrtOverTwo k is the project model of\n, used in the  branch\ninside the standard quadratic field.\n\nCoordinate API for . The project-owned order embeds\ninjectively into , has\nthe classical norm formula , and decomposes elements\ninto their integer and square-root coordinates.\n\nCoordinate API for the half-integral order. The order\n embeds into the standard field, has norm formula\n, and recognizes half-integer coordinates by the same-parity\ncondition.\n\nTrace and norm of half-integer coordinates. The element\n has trace  and the expected quadratic norm formula,\nwhich is the arithmetic input for the integrality normal-form proof.\n\nThe non- ring-of-integers branch\n(ringOfIntegers_equiv_zsqrtd_of_mod_four_ne_one). If  is squarefree,\n, and , then the ring of integers of\n is equivalent to\n.\n\nThe  ring-of-integers branch\n(ringOfIntegers_equiv_zOnePlusSqrtOverTwo_of_mod_four_eq_one). If\n, then the ring of integers of\n is equivalent to\n.\n\nThe ring-of-integers classification for quadratic fields\n(ringOfIntegers_classification). The theorem packages\nthe non- branch and\nthe  branch into one dichotomy.\n\nIntegral normal forms in . The proof runs through\nhalf-integer normal forms: integral elements land in the correct explicit order\naccording to the same mod-4 branch, supplying\nthe non- branch and\nthe  branch.\n\nTransport of the ring-of-integers classification to abstract quadratic fields\n(exists_ringOfIntegers_classification_of_quadraticField). After\nthe standard-model classification, the result\ntransports through the classification by\nsquarefree parameters.\n\nNorm-one criteria for units in the two quadratic orders. The explicit\ninteger-ring models  and\n have norm-based unit\ncriteria, used again in the unit-theory chapter.\n\nNorm laws and integer-coordinate norm formulas\n(norm_mul, norm_one, RingOfIntegers.norm_zsqrtd,\nRingOfIntegers.norm_zOnePlusSqrtOverTwo). The norm is multiplicative with\n, and on the explicit integer orders it has the closed-form\ncoordinate expressions  and . The\n variants confirm the same formulas after\nembedding into the standard field.\n\nThe norm of an integral element is again integral\n(RingOfIntegers.norm_mem_zsqrtd, norm_mem_zOnePlusSqrtOverTwo,\nnorm_mem_ringOfIntegers). The norm restricts from  to\n, but its values on integer-order elements lie in\n, and the same restriction holds for arbitrary\nring-of-integers elements — the input needed\nfor bounded-norm class representatives.\n\n","context":"Quadratic Number Fields Blueprint","header":"3. Rings of integers","id":"/Rings-of-integers/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Rings-of-integers"},"/Units/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Units":{"contents":"\n\nPell-type equations for quadratic units (IsPellSolution). The predicate\nIsPellSolution d n x y records the equation .\nThis is the bridge between coordinate arithmetic in\n and units.\n\nMultiplication of Pell solutions (IsPellSolution.mul).\nPell solutions multiply according to the\nnorm multiplication law in .\n\nFrom Pell equations to units. A norm-one Pell solution gives a unit of\n, and a norm-minus-one solution\ndoes the same after applying the dedicated construction. The nonzero\nsquare-root coordinate proves the resulting unit is nontrivial.\n\nA coordinate element  is a unit exactly when the corresponding\nPell equation has norm  or . This is\nthe coordinate counterpart of\nthe norm-based unit criterion.\n\nFor , the only units of \nare . The proof specializes\nthe Pell-equation unit criterion to imaginary\nparameters.\n\nThe analogous imaginary result holds in\nthe half-integer order for parameters ,\nspecializing the norm-based unit criterion to\nthe  branch.\n\nExceptional imaginary-unit classifications. The Gaussian and Eisenstein-type\nedge cases are handled separately from\nthe  statement and\nthe half-integral imaginary statement.\n\nFundamental units in real quadratic orders (IsFundamentalUnit). This is the\nproject predicate for a generator of the infinite part of a real quadratic unit\ngroup, modulo sign and inverse ambiguity. It\nabstracts the Pell-equation unit description into a\npredicate usable across coordinate models.\n\nTransport of fundamental units across ring equivalences\n(IsFundamentalUnit.map_ringEquiv). Fundamental-unit\nstructure transports across ring equivalences, matching the project's broader\ntransport-first\narchitecture and aligning with\nthe ring-equivalence shadow of the classification.\n\nSymmetries of the fundamental-unit predicate. Being fundamental is invariant\nunder inverse, sign, and monoid equivalence, matching the classical ambiguity\nof a generator of the infinite cyclic part of a real quadratic unit group.\n\nFundamental-unit conventions for finite unit groups. In imaginary cases with\nonly torsion units, the blueprint records the completed fundamental-unit\nstatements for , the half-integral order, and the\nGaussian and Eisenstein exceptional units.\n\nExplicit nontrivial unit candidates (HasExplicitUnitCandidate). The predicate\nrecords a nonzero Pell solution of norm  or , hence a concrete\ncandidate for a nontrivial unit of\n.\n\nExplicit Pell solutions for the classical families  and\n. These completed formulas provide the unit candidates used by\nthe Richaud-Degert family nodes.\n\nExplicit unit candidates in the four Pell families. The library packages\nthe concrete solutions as\nnontrivial unit candidates for\n, , , and .\n\nExplicit unit candidates for the  Richaud-Degert families. The theorem\nidentifies the parameter as  and covers shifts\n by reusing\nthe four Pell families.\n\nFrom an explicit Pell candidate to a nontrivial unit\n(HasExplicitUnitCandidate.exists_unit_ne_one). A completed candidate produces\na unit of  different from both\n and .\n\nThe future real-quadratic unit pipeline should connect continued fractions and\nPell solutions to explicit\nfundamental units.\n\n","context":"Quadratic Number Fields Blueprint","header":"7. Units","id":"/Units/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Units"}});